# Chapter 11 — Collision

Your things move now — by physics, by tween, by a button in your hand. But so far
they move *past* each other like ghosts. The hero glides straight through the
coin; the ball sails through the wall. Nothing happens when two things share the
same spot, because nothing is *checking*.

That check is collision, and it is the single most important question in a game:
*are these two things touching right now?* Answer it and everything opens up —
the coin gets grabbed, the wall stops the hero, the enemy lands a hit. This
chapter is that one question, asked well.

## Two things, not touching

Spawn two things far apart and ask whether they overlap. `entity/overlaps?` takes
two things and answers *do their boxes share any space?* — and when they are on
opposite corners, they plainly do not:

```motoi
(require 'game/world)
(world/gravity! 0 0)
(define hero (world/spawn 'hero 10 10))
(define coin (world/spawn 'coin 90 90))
(display (entity/overlaps? hero coin))
(newline)
```

```
#f
```

The hero sits at `(10, 10)`, the coin way off at `(90, 90)`, and `#f` is the
honest answer: their boxes are nowhere near each other. Every thing in the world
has an invisible box around it, and collision is nothing more than asking whether
two of those boxes touch. Right now, they do not.

## Two things, touching

Put them on the same spot and ask again. Now the boxes sit right on top of each
other, so they overlap completely:

```motoi
(require 'game/world)
(world/gravity! 0 0)
(define hero (world/spawn 'hero 40 40))
(define coin (world/spawn 'coin 40 40))
(display (entity/overlaps? hero coin))
(newline)
```

```
#t
```

Same position, so the boxes share all their space, and `entity/overlaps?` says
`#t`. That is the whole idea: `#f` when the boxes miss, `#t` when they touch. You
do not have to compute distances or corners yourself — you ask the one question
and the console checks the boxes for you.

## Move into a collision

Here is where the whole book comes together. A collision is not usually set up by
hand — it *happens*, because something moved into something else. Start the hero
apart from the coin, give it a velocity, and let the tick carry it in:

```motoi
(require 'game/world)
(world/gravity! 0 0)
(define hero (world/spawn 'hero 0 10))
(define coin (world/spawn 'coin 20 10))
(display (entity/overlaps? hero coin))
(newline)
(entity/set-vel! hero 5 0)
(world/step)
(world/step)
(display (entity/overlaps? hero coin))
(newline)
```

```
#f
#t
```

Read the two answers. At the start the hero is at `(0, 10)` and the coin at
`(20, 10)` — apart, so `#f`. You give the hero a velocity of `5` and step the
world twice; the tick from Chapter 5 slides it to `(10, 10)`, close enough that
the boxes now touch — `#t`. **This is a game happening.** Velocity (Chapter 5),
carried by the tick, produces a collision (this chapter) you can detect. Motion
and touching are the two halves, and here they meet.

## Collision is a question you build on

The `#t`/`#f` that `entity/overlaps?` gives back is not the end — it is the
*start* of your game's logic. Feed it to an `if`, and the touch becomes an event:

```motoi
(require 'game/world)
(world/gravity! 0 0)
(define hero (world/spawn 'hero 40 40))
(define coin (world/spawn 'coin 40 40))
(display (if (entity/overlaps? hero coin) 'grabbed 'not-yet))
(newline)
```

```
grabbed
```

The hero and coin overlap, so the `if` takes its first branch and the program
says `grabbed`. Swap the coin far away and the same code would say `not-yet`.
This is the shape of every pickup, every hit, every door: *check the overlap,
then decide what happens.* On a live console you run this check every frame, and
the instant the boxes touch, your `grabbed` branch fires — a sound, a score, a
vanished coin.

## What you just proved

You asked the one question a game lives on. You saw two things apart answer `#f`
and two things together answer `#t`; you moved a thing into another with velocity
and the tick, and watched the answer flip from `#f` to `#t` mid-motion; and you
wired that answer into an `if` so a touch became the word `grabbed`. Every program
ran and told you the plain truth about two boxes in space.

You now have every moving part of a game: a screen to draw on, a heartbeat to move
things, buttons to steer, sound to react, animation to polish, and collision to
make touches matter. What is left is to *arrange* it all — to place a whole level
of walls and coins and enemies at once. That arrangement is the scene, and it is
the next chapter.

> **Tip**
>
> `entity/overlaps?` checks boxes, not shapes. A round coin and a round hero still
> collide as squares — their invisible boxes touch a little before the circles
> would. For most games that feels fine, and it is fast. If a collision ever fires
> a touch too early, it is almost always the box being bigger than the art inside
> it; shrink the thing, not your expectations.

> **Warning**
>
> A collision is only true for the frame you check it. If the hero passes through
> the coin between two steps — moving so fast it skips over the overlap — you can
> miss the touch entirely. Check every frame with a small enough step, or the
> fast-moving bullet will tunnel straight through the wall. When a hit refuses to
> register, suspect a thing moving farther per step than its own box is wide.

